@node	0.2, 0.3, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Expressions, values, and types

@noindent
Every value in this book's OCaml has one of a small set of primitive types:
@code{int}, @code{float}, @code{bool}, @code{string}, and @code{unit}, the
type with exactly one value, written @code{()}, that stands for ``nothing
interesting to return.'' A literal number is an expression, exactly as it is
in Scheme, and the interpreter reports its type along with its value:

@example
# 486;;
- : int = 486
@end example

@noindent
Arithmetic combines expressions the way Scheme's @code{(+ 137 349)} does, but
prefix notation becomes infix, and each operator works on exactly one type:

@example
# 5 + 3 + 4;;
- : int = 12
# 10 - 9;;
- : int = 1
# (2 * 4) + (4 - 6);;
- : int = 6
# 10 / 5;;
- : int = 2
# 21 * 35;;
- : int = 735
@end example

@noindent
@code{/} is integer division; it truncates, and @code{mod} recovers the
remainder:

@example
# 10 / 3;;
- : int = 3
# 10 mod 3;;
- : int = 1
@end example

@subsubheading Two number types, never mixed silently

Scheme's numeric tower quietly moves between exact and inexact numbers;
@code{(+ 2.7 10)} is @code{12.7} with no ceremony. OCaml keeps @code{int} and
@code{float} apart, with distinct operators for each: @code{+}, @code{-},
@code{*}, and @code{/} work only on @code{int}, and @code{+.}, @code{-.},
@code{*.}, and @code{/.} work only on @code{float}. There is no automatic
promotion between them:

@example
# 10.0 /. 3.0;;
- : float = 3.33333333333333348
# 1 +. 1.0;;
Line 1, characters 0-1:
1 | 1 +. 1.0;;
    ^
Error: The constant 1 has type int but an expression was expected of type
         float
Hint: Did you mean 1.?
@end example

@noindent
That error is not friction; it is the type checker catching, before the
program runs, exactly the class of mistake that in a dynamically typed
language surfaces only when the wrong branch finally executes. The hint is
literal advice: @code{1.} is the @code{float} literal for one, spelled with a
trailing dot. Crossing from @code{int} to @code{float} is always a deliberate
function call, most often @code{Float.of_int}:

@example
# Float.of_int 5;;
- : float = 5.
@end example

@noindent
and the reverse, @code{int_of_float}, truncates toward zero exactly as
@code{/} does:

@example
# int_of_float 3.7;;
- : int = 3
@end example

@noindent
This book's numeric convention fixes which of the two is used where:
@code{int} for counters, indices, and exact arithmetic;
@code{float} for anything that can be irrational, such as square roots and
iterative approximations. The two are never mixed in one expression without
an explicit conversion, and that rule has no exceptions in this book's code.

@subsubheading Strings, booleans, and comparisons

Strings are a fourth primitive type, concatenated with @code{^}:

@example
# string_of_int 42;;
- : string = "42"
# "lis" ^ "p";;
- : string = "lisp"
# String.length "scheme";;
- : int = 6
@end example

@noindent
@code{bool} is a fifth, with the two values @code{true} and @code{false}.
Comparison operators return @code{bool} rather than a truthy/falsy value of
some other type, and @code{not} negates:

@example
# 1 < 2;;
- : bool = true
# not (1 = 2);;
- : bool = true
@end example

@noindent
@code{=} is structural equality on any two values of the same type,
including two @code{int}s here; Scheme's three-way split between @code{eq?},
@code{eqv?}, and @code{equal?} appears later, in section 0.6, once mutable
data gives identity a reason to differ from equality.

@subsubheading Named values, briefly

@code{let} names a value, and every named value carries the type the
compiler inferred for it, never a type the programmer merely hoped for. Two
small definitions from this section's running example, reproduced in
@file{ch0/lib/sec_0_2.ml}, show a converting function and a
comparison-producing one side by side:

@example
# let average_of_two_ints a b = Float.of_int (a + b) /. 2.0;;
val average_of_two_ints : int -> int -> float = <fun>
# average_of_two_ints 5 3;;
- : float = 4.
# let warm_enough celsius = celsius >= 18.0;;
val warm_enough : float -> bool = <fun>
# warm_enough 20.0;;
- : bool = true
# warm_enough 10.0;;
- : bool = false
# let greet name = "hello, " ^ name ^ "!";;
val greet : string -> string = <fun>
# greet "sicp";;
- : string = "hello, sicp!"
@end example

@noindent
@code{average_of_two_ints} adds two @code{int}s first, then crosses to
@code{float} in the one place the division needs it; the type checker would
reject the addition itself becoming a @code{float} operation, since @code{a}
and @code{b} are still @code{int} at that point. Section 0.3 covers
@code{let} and function definitions properly; this section's point is
narrower: every value here has exactly one type, decided at compile time, and
that type is what the interpreter reports on the line above every value.

@subsubheading Exercises

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.1}Exercise 0.1:} Translate each of the five Scheme
interactions below into OCaml and reproduce it in utop, writing each response
exactly as the toplevel prints it, inferred type included: @code{(+ 2 (* 4
6))}; the @code{square} definition together with the call @code{(square 7)};
@code{(if (> 4 11) 4 11)}, generalized to a two-argument @code{larger}
function and then specialized back to @code{larger_of_4_and_11}; and the
anonymous square @code{((lambda (x) (* x x)) 5)}. Name the six results
@code{sum_with_product}, @code{square}, @code{seven_squared}, @code{larger},
@code{larger_of_4_and_11}, and @code{anonymous_square}.
@end quotation

@noindent
A scaffold for it lives in @file{ch0/exercises/ex_0_01.mli}; the reference
solution is @file{ch0/solutions/ex_0_01.ml}, with the reasoning in
@file{ch0/solutions/ex_0_01.md} and its contract pinned by
@file{ch0/test/test_solutions.ml}.
